  data:  # c515
    kind: work_item  # c515
    title: "Elixir behaviours unmodelled: @callback emits no symbol and @behaviour emits no edge"  # c515
    status: todo_hard  # c515
    priority: 2  # c515
    parent:  # c515
    tags:  # c515
    - elixir  # c515
    - analyzer  # c515
    - linker_gap  # c515
    isbefore: []  # c515
    duplicate_of: []  # c515
    not_duplicate_of: []  # c515
    pr_ref:  # c515
    description: "**Elixir behaviours are the language's interface mechanism and are wholly unmodelled.** Measured by the interface-dispatch parity column on:\n\n    defmodule Shape do\n      @callback area() :: integer\n    end\n\n    defmodule Square do\n      @behaviour Shape\n      def area, do: 1\n    end\n\nEmitted:\n\n    module 'Shape'      <- no members\n    module 'Square'\n    function 'Square.area'\n\nTwo independent gaps:\n\n1. **`@callback area() :: integer` produces no symbol at all.** The behaviour's required function does not exist in the graph, so there is nothing to dispatch FROM. (Directly parallel to rust traits before WI-duguk, and to cpp's dropped abstract methods.)\n2. **`@behaviour Shape` produces no edge.** No `implements`, `extends` or `inherits` links the implementing module to the behaviour, so even with (1) fixed there would be no relationship to traverse.\n\nBoth are needed; either alone leaves the cell red.\n\n**Why this matters more than one parity cell.** `@behaviour` is how Elixir/OTP expresses `GenServer`, `Supervisor`, `Application` and every custom contract. A codebase's entire OTP structure is currently invisible as a type relationship. `linkers/otp.py` exists and covers OTP dispatch by other means — check what it already recovers before scoping this, since the overlap may be substantial and the gap smaller than it looks.\n\nHole recorded as a strict xfail in `test_interface_dispatch_parity.py`."  # c515
    fields: {}  # c515
- op: discuss  # 48a4
  at: '2026-10-01T00:56:46Z'  # 48a4
  by: agent  # 48a4
  actor: jgstern_agent  # 48a4
  clock: 2  # 48a4
  nonce: 48a4  # 48a4
  message: "PREMISE CHECK 2026-09-30 (dev 134a06d505): CURRENT-WITH-UPDATES.\n\nRepro re-run: the parity CASES[\"elixir\"] fixture emits module Shape (no members), module Square,\nfunction Square.area, file node; no inheritance edge, no dispatches_to. Both gaps hold.\n- Gap 1 is DELIBERATE today: elixir.py:505 _TYPESPEC_ATTRIBUTES includes \"callback\"/\"macrocallback\"\n  and the module docstring (elixir.py:61) says @type/@callback \"are types and emit nothing\"\n  (_inside_typespec, WI-dokib; bcb701b655). That rule is about CALL edges inside a typespec; a fix\n  that mints a symbol for @callback must not undo it.\n- Gap 2: elixir.py:870 _extract_behaviour_callbacks DOES parse `@behaviour X` and `use X`, but only\n  for X in the hard-coded BEHAVIOUR_CALLBACKS table (elixir.py:110: GenServer, Supervisor, Agent, Task,\n  Phoenix.LiveView/LiveComponent/Component, Plug(+Builder/Router), WebSock, WebSockex). For those it\n  emits module -> callback dispatches_to (evidence behaviour_callback). Verified on a fixture:\n  `@behaviour Plug` + def init/call -> dispatches_to MyPlug -> MyPlug.init and MyPlug -> MyPlug.call.\n  A custom `@behaviour Shape` hits nothing.\nUPDATE to the description: \"A codebase's entire OTP structure is currently invisible\" overstates it --\ncallbacks of the 15 table behaviours are rooted (as dispatches_to from the module), and linkers/otp.py\nbridges GenServer.call/cast sites to handle_call/handle_cast. What IS absent: any type relationship\n(no implements edge from a module to its behaviour) and anything for custom behaviours. The fix should\nreuse the existing @behaviour parse in _extract_behaviour_callbacks rather than add a second one, and\ndecide what kind a @callback symbol gets (cf. WI-ganah, @attributes as kind=variable).\ninheritance.py:550-556 notes elixir writes no base_classes / included_modules meta."  # 48a4
- op: update  # b40b
  at: '2026-10-01T01:34:51Z'  # b40b
  by: agent  # b40b
  actor: jgstern_agent  # b40b
  clock: 3  # b40b
  nonce: b40b  # b40b
  set:  # b40b
    description: "**Elixir behaviours are the language's interface mechanism and are not modelled as a type relationship; custom behaviours are not modelled at all.** Measured by the interface-dispatch parity column on:\n\n    defmodule Shape do\n      @callback area() :: integer\n    end\n\n    defmodule Square do\n      @behaviour Shape\n      def area, do: 1\n    end\n\nEmitted:\n\n    module 'Shape'      <- no members\n    module 'Square'\n    function 'Square.area'\n\nTwo independent gaps:\n\n1. **`@callback area() :: integer` produces no symbol at all.** The behaviour's required function does not exist in the graph, so there is nothing to dispatch FROM. (Directly parallel to rust traits before WI-duguk, and to cpp's dropped abstract methods.) This is currently DELIBERATE: `elixir.py` `_TYPESPEC_ATTRIBUTES` lists `callback`/`macrocallback`, and `_inside_typespec` makes a typespec body emit nothing (WI-dokib). That rule exists to stop CALL edges from type references inside `@spec`/`@type`/`@callback`; minting a symbol for the `@callback` itself must not undo it.\n2. **`@behaviour Shape` produces no edge.** No `implements`, `extends` or `inherits` links the implementing module to the behaviour, so even with (1) fixed there would be no relationship to traverse. `elixir.py` `_extract_behaviour_callbacks` already parses `@behaviour X` and `use X`, but acts only when X is in the hard-coded `BEHAVIOUR_CALLBACKS` table (12 framework behaviours: GenServer, Supervisor, Agent, Task, Phoenix.LiveView/LiveComponent/Component, Plug, Plug.Builder, Plug.Router, WebSock, WebSockex), and then emits module -> callback `dispatches_to` (evidence `behaviour_callback`), not an inheritance edge. A custom behaviour like `Shape` gets nothing. The fix should extend that parse rather than add a second one. `linkers/inheritance.py` records that elixir writes neither `base_classes` nor `included_modules`.\n\nBoth are needed; either alone leaves the cell red.\n\n**Why this matters more than one parity cell.** `@behaviour` is how Elixir/OTP expresses `GenServer`, `Supervisor`, `Application` and every custom contract. What already exists: callbacks of the 12 table behaviours are rooted by the module -> callback `dispatches_to` edges above, and `linkers/otp.py` links `GenServer.call`/`cast` sites to `handle_call`/`handle_cast`. What is absent: any module -> behaviour type relationship (for every behaviour, including the 12), and any modelling of custom behaviours (`@callback` symbols, implementor -> callback dispatch). Decide what kind a `@callback` symbol gets (cf. WI-ganah, module @attributes as kind=variable).\n\nHole recorded as a strict xfail in `test_interface_dispatch_parity.py`.\n\nRevised 2026-09-30 from the premise check (see discussion)."  # b40b
